 DDiiaaggnnoossttiiccss    

     If  the  translator  gets  hung  up on an expression it will
     usually  abort.   A  message of cause is printed, but it may
     not  mean  much.  The message "flex input buffer overflowed"
     may  be  related to strings in the file.  Check the C output
     file for the last token the translator was able to read.  

     "method  is  generic"  means that no description of a method
     selector  was  found  in  the  method  interface  definition
     files.   This  warning  isn't  fatal;  it just means that no
     type  casting  of  the  return value will be performed.  The
     compiler  will probably also complain if the return value is
     assigned to a non-id type variable.  

     If  there  is a type disagreement between any interface file
     and  a  method  call,  either  the warnings "mismatch return
     type"  or  "mismatch  parameter  type"  will  be  displayed.
     Currently,  the storeage class of each parameter must match;
     this  should be relaxed in the future.  Fatal errors include
     unable  to  system()  _c_p_p_.   If  the preprocessor returns an
     error value, then translation stops.  


 CCrreeddiittss    

     The  preprocessor  provided  with  the system is from DECUS,
     and  was  ported to the Amiga by Fred Fish.  The program has
     been  tweeked  to  accept  "//"  comments,  and recogize the
     #import  directive.  This makes it incompatible with regular
     AT&T UNIX _c_p_p program, but more like GNU  _c_p_p_.  
     The  Objective  C  Translator  system  is based on Dr. Cox's
     Objective-C,  version  2,  as  described in his book, _O_b_j_e_c_t
     _O_r_i_e_n_t_e_d     _P_r_o_g_r_a_m_m_i_n_g_:    _A_n    _E_v_o_l_u_t_i_o_n_a_r_y    _A_p_p_r_o_a_c_h_,
     Addison-Wesley,  1986.  Extensions are from versions 2 and 3
     of  _o_b_j_c from NeXT, which includes @abstract and some of the
     class  methods.   The  system  was  developed using _f_l_e_x and
     _b_i_s_o_n  from the Free Software Foundation.  Only _f_l_e_x is used
     in the release version.  

     Please  abide  by the software licences of the Free Software
     Foundation  and DECUS with their respective products.  DECUS
     is an independent user's group.  

     The  translator  is  about  120K  of source; the classes are
     another  60K  of code.  The system was developed on an Amiga
     1000   with   1.5   Meg  memory  using  Manx  C  vers  3.6A.
     Especially   enjoyable   are   the   Amiga's  multi-tasking,
     windowing,  and  dynamicly  sizing  RAM  disk.  Stu Ferguson
     helped  spot deficiencies in the whole system.  Thanks to my
     ex-manager  Greg  O'Feish for allowing me time to bring this
     system to life.  


